# Alert Rule Monitoring   

Azure quota alert rule monitoring is essential to manage cloud infrastructure and provides customers with timely alerts and insights into usage trends and anomalies, which allows them to take proactive steps to optimize their cloud resources and avoid or resolve potential issues. 

This document provides a conceptual overview about alert rules and monitoring for quotas used in the [Azure portal](https://ms.portal.azure.com/). 

## Alert rules 

Azure quota alerts rules are mechanisms our customers create to successfully manage their cloud resources, such as storage capacity, and other indicators of performance and usage. Alert rules are created, by configuring specific usage scenarios such a set capacity usage of 80%, with frequency of checking every 15 minutes, with conditions to fire a set severity-level alert when this occurs.

When a customer's usage of a particular resource exceeds or approaches the defined threshold, an alert is generated, based on the defined parameters to indicate that the customer is nearing their quota limit. for more information, see [Create an alert rule]<--add x-ref>.

## Monitor alert rules 

Azure quota alert rule monitoring works by using automated tools that track resources and thresholds defined by a quota alert rule, that the customer creates based on Azure specifications. These metrics can include usage consumption error rates, and other indicators of performance and usage.

When a metric reaches the threshold defined in an alert rule, such as 80% usage capacity, the monitoring system triggers an alert, which is then sent to the owner or contributor-level users and teams for action. The alert can be delivered by email, SMS, or mobile app with details about the metrics that triggered the alert. Customers can monitor their quotas in real time and can view quota details on Azure quota dashboard. 

### Log Analytics 

Log analytics is a tool to help organizations optimize their cloud infrastructure. Alert rule log analytics collects data to identify information such as the number of alerts generated, the frequency and severity of alerts, the time it takes to respond to alerts, and any actions taken in response to alerts. By analyzing this data, customers can identify trends and patterns that may indicate issues with the alert rules, such as false positives or missed alerts. 

For more info, see the Logging Analytics section in [Monitor alert rules](-- add xref). 

